WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:07.670
Depictions of the future often imagine vast
technological structures that shatter the

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:10.210
mind with their sheer scale.

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:15.570
In science fiction, these hypothetical objects
are called ‘megastructures’ — and although

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:21.080
it would take countless technological leaps
to construct one, cosmic-scale Megastructures

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.240
may one day become scientific fact.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.380
But what kinds of structures are future humans
likely to create?

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.410
What would it be like to live on one?

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:34.470
And what’s the science behind these speculative
wonders?

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:39.890
One of my favorite sci fi illustrators, Neil
Blevins, has been hard at work answering these

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:45.860
very questions: creating a visual encyclopedia
of Megastructures that both explains the science

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:51.280
behind the objects, and includes paintings
and diagrams to bring them to life.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:56.540
Blevins is planning to release the full encyclopedia
next year, which you can order using the links

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.420
below, and also follow and support his work.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:04.059
And for this special entry into the archive,
Neil Blevins has given me permission to show

00:01:04.059 --> 00:01:10.050
you a sneak peak of various megastructures:
from orbiting space habitats to solar system

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.390
spanning stellar engineering projects.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:18.159
So, let’s embark on a journey through a
myriad of possible futures — and experience

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:22.360
the mind-shattering scale of megastructures.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:29.380
Imagine an artificial ring of unfathomable
size, large enough to maintain an artificial

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.310
atmosphere and habitable land.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:37.979
This is a Ringworld, an awe-inspiring theoretical
structure, and one you might have seen across

00:01:37.979 --> 00:01:39.780
science fiction.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:44.220
Ringworlds have come in different shapes and
sizes throughout various forms of media — but

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:48.370
the Ringworld Blevins imagines is unfathomably
vast.

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:53.030
His model is of a ‘stellar scale’ ring:
meaning it is large enough to encircle the

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.930
sun — potentially millions of miles across.

00:01:56.930 --> 00:02:01.940
Building a structure of this size would require
an incredible amount of raw material, perhaps

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:05.380
taken from asteroids within and beyond our
solar system.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:10.889
And to simulate earth-like gravity, the entire
ringworld would need to spin.

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:14.569
If that sounds surprising, you’ve likely
experienced something similar if you’ve

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:19.489
ever been pushed against the edge of a merry
go-round while it spun at high speeds.

00:02:19.489 --> 00:02:24.900
Our rotating Ringworld would produce the feeling
of gravity using, essentially, the same principle

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:29.079
— meaning that walking on the surface of
one would feel pretty normal.

00:02:29.079 --> 00:02:34.579
A ringworld of this size could even have nights
as well as days, through the use of a segmented

00:02:34.579 --> 00:02:39.760
inner ring that rotates faster to cast shadows
on the ring’s surface.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:44.910
Ideally, life on a ringworld would be a lot
like life on Earth: just a bit roomier, as

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:51.829
a stellar-scale ringworld could have over
a million times the area of our own planet.

00:02:51.829 --> 00:02:55.049
But rings aren’t the only possible shape
for a megastructure.

00:02:55.049 --> 00:02:58.930
An even wilder design is that of a Bernal
Sphere.

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:03.659
From its industrial exterior, this sphere
might not look like a pleasant place to live.

00:03:03.659 --> 00:03:09.309
On the hollow interior, however, a spectacular
colony could be supported — which Blevins

00:03:09.309 --> 00:03:11.220
has rendered in detail.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:16.609
Dwellings could be built on the inside surface,
with gravity simulated via the now-familiar

00:03:16.609 --> 00:03:18.849
method of a steady rotation.

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:24.139
Due to the spherical shape, however, gravity
at the poles would be quite a bit lower, and

00:03:24.139 --> 00:03:29.489
hence could be the location of spaceship docking
ports and zero gravity manufacturing.

00:03:29.489 --> 00:03:34.480
Recreational activities such as low-gravity
swimming could also be held in this area,

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.609
which sounds strangely fun.

00:03:37.609 --> 00:03:40.559
But there are even more unique possible shapes.

00:03:40.559 --> 00:03:45.109
One of my personal favorite megastructures
is called a Hoopworld… it kind of looks

00:03:45.109 --> 00:03:48.669
like an enormous, habitable donut floating
in space.

00:03:48.669 --> 00:03:53.499
As cool as this looks, Belvins imagines the
Hoopworld would have some potential drawbacks,

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:58.669
as the shape of this megastructure would cause
stronger simulated gravity on the inner equator,

00:03:58.669 --> 00:04:01.229
and weaker gravity on the outer one.

00:04:01.229 --> 00:04:06.170
Life on a Hoopworld would likely depend on
its position relative to the sun, as tipping

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:10.959
the structure toward or away from the sun
could create seasons, just like our Earth’s

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:14.349
tilt is responsible for the seasons on our
own planet.

00:04:14.349 --> 00:04:18.870
Personally, I think life on the space-donut
sounds worth the hurdles…

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:23.210
Yet megastructures wouldn’t necessarily
have to be built in space.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:28.080
There is a vast portion of Earth which, much
like the cosmos, we currently inhabit very

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.560
little of: our oceans.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:35.040
Since over 70% percent of Earth’s surface
is covered in water, it makes sense we might

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.639
one day build structures that allow us to
live under the waves.

00:04:39.639 --> 00:04:44.460
Sci fi artists from past decades have often
imagined undersea cities that look much like

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:48.620
the ones we have on land, just encased in
glass bubbles.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:54.610
Blevins’s model of an underwater megastructure,
however, is far more unique, and more efficient.

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:59.380
While not a ‘city’ as we know them today,
this megastructure is a series of interconnected

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:04.219
modules: which could be more easily isolated
and repaired should a leak ever occur.

00:05:04.219 --> 00:05:10.460
It would also make expansion fairly straightforward,
as new modules could be added at any time.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:15.069
A curious option for habitation…

00:05:15.069 --> 00:05:18.039
Megastructures might not be used for just
habitation, however.

00:05:18.039 --> 00:05:23.561
They could also be built to obtain resources:
which is the purpose of the colossal Gas Giant

00:05:23.561 --> 00:05:25.160
Refinery.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:30.430
Gas giants like Jupiter contain huge amounts
of hydrogen and helium, raw elements that

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:33.949
might be fundamental to an interplanetary
civilization.

00:05:33.949 --> 00:05:39.789
Today, we have no way to access these elements,
despite knowing of their existence.

00:05:39.789 --> 00:05:45.050
Blevins conceptualizes how one day; however,
we could suspend a series of structures in

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:49.830
the atmosphere of Jupiter to harvest a safe
portion of these gases.

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:54.719
Lowering a megastructure into a planet as
unpredictable as Jupiter would be a daring

00:05:54.719 --> 00:05:58.490
endeavor, and would likely require multiple
steps.

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:03.990
The refineries Blevins has designed work via
balloons filled with buoyant gas, which uses

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:08.819
a portion of the extracted hydrogen and helium
to raise and lower the refinery.

00:06:08.819 --> 00:06:13.189
An orbital vehicle would then transport the
extracted material elsewhere.

00:06:13.189 --> 00:06:18.120
A true feat of engineering — though given
humans’ tendencies to damage our own environment,

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:21.710
it would need to be done carefully.

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:25.590
But raw elements aren’t the only resource
future humans might depend on.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:30.870
It’s also possible we’d still be dependent
on farms for food, even as we venture out

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:32.160
into space.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:37.669
These huge, hexagonal structures are aptly
named ‘space farms,’ and Blevins imagines

00:06:37.669 --> 00:06:40.389
they’d solve that very problem.

00:06:40.389 --> 00:06:44.449
Within these vast structures, the atmosphere
could be controlled to produce the highest

00:06:44.449 --> 00:06:49.750
crop yield and a set of mirrors could reflect
real starlight to create a fake day/night

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:50.750
cycle.

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:54.479
With this model, walking among these crops
would likely be a lot like strolling through

00:06:54.479 --> 00:06:55.889
a regular farm.

00:06:55.889 --> 00:07:00.610
Of course, it might also be more practical
to grow plants in more controlled, greenhouse-like

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:03.509
zero-g environments within the structure.

00:07:03.509 --> 00:07:08.529
Whatever the case, this is one Megastructure
that seems particularly useful.

00:07:08.529 --> 00:07:14.129
Yet there are other, darker potential purposes
behind building a megastructure: to create

00:07:14.129 --> 00:07:16.629
a cosmos-shaking superweapon.

00:07:16.629 --> 00:07:21.660
The most famous destructive megastructure
in fiction is probably the classic Death Star.

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:28.749
Belvins portrays an even larger, more terrifying
superweapon, however: a Dyson Laser.

00:07:28.749 --> 00:07:33.169
Essentially a swarm of satellites surrounding
a sun, this theoretical device would focus

00:07:33.169 --> 00:07:38.719
the energy of a star into a beam capable of
destroying the atmosphere of a planet, or

00:07:38.719 --> 00:07:41.280
even destroying the planet itself.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:46.419
This is one megastructure we have to hope
is never constructed…

00:07:46.419 --> 00:07:51.870
The last Megastructure to cover, however,
is one of the most awe-inspiring and truly

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:52.880
unique.

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:57.909
This titanic artificial landscape is called
an Alderson Disk — and is essentially a

00:07:57.909 --> 00:07:59.610
giant ‘platter’ of land.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:05.539
And with a radius equivalent to Mars’s orbit,
the Alderson Disk is the single largest structure

00:08:05.539 --> 00:08:07.000
in this entry.

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:10.039
Life on an Alderson disk would be one of extremes.

00:08:10.039 --> 00:08:15.099
The region closest to the sun would be an
arid region millions of times larger than

00:08:15.099 --> 00:08:16.889
any desert of earth.

00:08:16.889 --> 00:08:22.400
Likewise, the region furthest from the sun
would likely be a frigid ice sheet far colder

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:24.750
than Earth’s polar regions.

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:29.999
Only in the central habitable zone would temperatures
reach moderate levels — but this habitable

00:08:29.999 --> 00:08:35.250
zone would be pretty roomy: millions of times
the space we have on earth.

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:39.640
However, in order to have earth-like gravity,
the disk would have to be several thousand

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:44.600
kilometers thick: which, combined with its
radius. would require more material than all

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:46.970
of the planets in our solar system… combined.

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:52.990
It would also have to be made of a hyper advanced
construction material to keep it from collapsing.

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:57.790
In other words, as amazing as an Alderson
disk looks, it’s not exactly a simple thing

00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:00.040
to actually construct…

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:05.790
Ultimately, Megastructures are incredible…
but they’re also a very distant aspiration.

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:10.149
If any of these amazing structures are in
our future, they’re too far away for us

00:09:10.149 --> 00:09:12.600
to depend on as a fallback.

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:17.320
For the time being, the only place in the
universe fit for long-term habitation is the

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:19.980
curious little rock we call Earth.

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:24.760
So, as we look to the horizon, we must also
remember the importance of what’s right

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:25.870
beneath our feet…

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:33.220
Blevins is releasing a full book on these
megastructures and many more very soon.

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:37.250
You can also follow and support Neil Blevins
using the links in the description.

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:40.550
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:45.199
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support and like, subscribe, and hit the notification

00:09:45.199 --> 00:09:48.350
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:49.700
See you in the next video.

